Chapter: GopSceneBackbuffer
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Works chapter GopScene

 GopScene renders into a back buffer and blits it to the desktop's content
 pane. The blit is the only step that reads both pitches -- destination
 rows sit stride apart on the panel, source rows sit w apart in the back
 buffer -- so it is the one place in the chapter a padded scanline can
 shear, and the ASUS TUF pads its scanlines: 1920 visible against a
 PixelsPerScanLine of 2048.

 200 visible against a 264-pixel scanline is the same shape small. No
 emulator feature is involved, because both pitches are ordinary
 arguments.

 Every source pixel carries its own row and column, so the assertion is
 where a pixel LANDED and not merely that something was painted. A
 width-stepping blit puts row 0 in the right place and every later row at
 r * 160 instead of r * 264, which is why row 0 is not evidence and the
 count over all 24 rows is.

 The sidebar columns, the taskbar rows and the 64 pad columns beyond the
 visible width are pre-filled with a sentinel and must still hold it. A
 blit that painted the whole panel would satisfy the landing count just as
 well, and the containment rows are what separate the two.

 The last row is the calibration and it is the reason to believe the rest:
 the same blit is asked for the visible pane width as the destination
 pitch, into a fresh panel, and the landing count is required to DISAGREE.
 Without it every row above would be satisfied by an instrument that could
 not tell the two pitches apart.

 Sabotaged against seed 6671C19A0F78F630 by stepping the destination rows
 by w: landing goes to 1 of 24, and the pane, sidebar and pad rows go to
 no. **The taskbar row does NOT move, and it is worth knowing why rather
 than reading it as a control that held.** A short-pitch blit writes no
 further than pixel 23 * 160 + 199, which is inside row 15 of a 264-pitch
 panel, so it cannot reach row 24 whatever it gets wrong. That row is a
 control against a blit that paints too MUCH, not against one that picks
 the wrong pitch, and the pad row is the one that catches the pitch on the
 containment side.

 We say:

Section: Geometry

  vis-w : Integer = 200
  fb-stride : Integer = 264
  fb-rows : Integer = 32
  sidebar : Integer = 40
  taskbar : Integer = 8
  cw : Integer = 160
  ch : Integer = 24
  sentinel : Integer = 12648430

Section: Panel

  fill-panel : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fill-panel (b) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let d = poke-32 b (i * 4) sentinel
    in fill-panel b (i + 1) n

 Row r, column c carries r * 1000 + c + 1, so no payload value collides
 with another and none of them is the sentinel.

  payload : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  payload (r) (c) = r * 1000 + c + 1

  fill-src-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fill-src-cols (b) (r) (c) =
    if c >= cw then 0
    else let d = poke-32 b ((r * cw + c) * 4) (payload r c)
    in fill-src-cols b r (c + 1)

  fill-src : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fill-src (b) (r) =
    if r >= ch then 0
    else let d = fill-src-cols b r 0
    in fill-src b (r + 1)

Section: Counting

 A row has landed when its first and its last pixel are both where the
 stride says they should be. One of the two alone would be satisfied by a
 blit that got the row origin right and the run length wrong.

  rows-landed : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  rows-landed (p) (r) (acc) =
    if r >= ch then acc
    else if peek-32 p ((r * fb-stride + sidebar) * 4) == payload r 0 & peek-32 p ((r * fb-stride + sidebar + cw - 1) * 4) == payload r (cw - 1) then rows-landed p (r + 1) (acc + 1)
    else rows-landed p (r + 1) acc

  count-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-cols (p) (x0) (y0) (rw) (y) (x) =
    if x >= rw then 0
    else let here = if peek-32 p (((y0 + y) * fb-stride + x0 + x) * 4) == sentinel then 1 else 0
    in here + count-cols p x0 y0 rw y (x + 1)

  count-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-rows (p) (x0) (y0) (rw) (rh) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= rh then acc
    else count-rows p x0 y0 rw rh (y + 1) (acc + count-cols p x0 y0 rw y 0)

  intact : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  intact (p) (x0) (y0) (rw) (rh) = count-rows p x0 y0 rw rh 0 0 == rw * rh

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let src = alloc-bytes (cw * ch * 4)
    in let fs = fill-src src 0
    in let panel = alloc-bytes (fb-stride * fb-rows * 4)
    in let fp = fill-panel panel 0 (fb-stride * fb-rows)
    in let b1 = gsc-blit-rows (panel + sidebar * 4) fb-stride src cw ch 0
    in let landed = rows-landed panel 0 0
    in let side-ok = intact panel 0 0 sidebar fb-rows
    in let task-ok = intact panel sidebar ch cw taskbar
    in let pad-ok = intact panel vis-w 0 (fb-stride - vis-w) fb-rows
    in let pane-kept = count-rows panel sidebar 0 cw ch 0 0
    in let bad = alloc-bytes (fb-stride * fb-rows * 4)
    in let fb2 = fill-panel bad 0 (fb-stride * fb-rows)
    in let b2 = gsc-blit-rows (bad + sidebar * 4) cw src cw ch 0
    in let landed-bad = rows-landed bad 0 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("rows land by stride    : " & integer-to-text landed & " of " & integer-to-text ch)
      print-line-uni ("pane fully painted     : " & yn (pane-kept == 0))
      print-line-uni ("sidebar untouched      : " & yn side-ok)
      print-line-uni ("taskbar untouched      : " & yn task-ok)
      print-line-uni ("pad columns untouched  : " & yn pad-ok)
      print-line-uni ("calibration disagrees  : " & yn (landed-bad < ch))
    end
  end

